WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.193
Here are seven keys

00:00:02.287 --> 00:00:05.600
inserted into a plain binary search

00:00:05.694 --> 00:00:09.290
tree in the order 4, 2, 6, 1

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:11.183
3, 5, 7.

00:00:11.183 --> 00:00:14.495
The tree is as bushy as it can be

00:00:14.495 --> 00:00:15.631
height 2.

00:00:15.631 --> 00:00:19.700
Every search walks at most two edges down.

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:22.029
Now insert the same seven

00:00:22.108 --> 00:00:25.116
keys in sorted order: 1, 2, 3

00:00:25.116 --> 00:00:26.145
up to 7.

00:00:26.224 --> 00:00:28.361
Each new key is larger than

00:00:28.441 --> 00:00:30.103
everything before it

00:00:30.103 --> 00:00:32.636
so it always goes to the right.

00:00:32.636 --> 00:00:35.406
The tree is a chain of height 6

00:00:35.406 --> 00:00:38.810
and searching for 7 visits every node.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:40.920
Same keys, same code

00:00:41.004 --> 00:00:43.266
and one tree is three times

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.193
taller than the other.

00:00:45.277 --> 00:00:48.378
This lecture is about a binary search

00:00:48.462 --> 00:00:51.227
tree that never lets this happen

00:00:51.227 --> 00:00:52.400
the AVL tree.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:56.009
Recall from Lecture 14: find

00:00:56.096 --> 00:00:58.432
insert and delete each walk

00:00:58.519 --> 00:01:00.509
one path from the root

00:01:00.509 --> 00:01:02.500
so each costs O(h)

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:05.529
where h is the height of the tree.

00:01:05.529 --> 00:01:07.260
How large can h be?

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:10.750
In the best case every level is full.

00:01:10.835 --> 00:01:13.551
A tree of height h holds at most

00:01:13.636 --> 00:01:16.945
2^(h + 1) - 1 nodes

00:01:16.945 --> 00:01:20.680
so h is at least the floor of log₂ n.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.329
A complete binary tree reaches that bound.

00:01:24.329 --> 00:01:28.063
In the worst case every node has one child

00:01:28.063 --> 00:01:29.930
and h is n - 1.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:33.245
And the worst case is not exotic.

00:01:33.245 --> 00:01:36.389
Keys often arrive in sorted order

00:01:36.389 --> 00:01:38.635
student IDs, timestamps

00:01:38.635 --> 00:01:40.792
an already sorted file.

00:01:40.792 --> 00:01:44.295
Sorted input builds exactly the chain

00:01:44.295 --> 00:01:47.440
and every operation costs O(n).

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:50.274
All the programs in this lecture

00:01:50.344 --> 00:01:51.824
share one node class.

00:01:51.824 --> 00:01:53.514
As in Lecture 14

00:01:53.585 --> 00:01:55.980
a node has an element and links to

00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:58.022
its left and right children.

00:01:58.022 --> 00:02:01.051
And it stores one more number: its height

00:02:01.122 --> 00:02:03.164
because an AVL tree needs the

00:02:03.235 --> 00:02:05.700
height of every node, all the time.

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:08.721
The constructor that takes two children

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:10.843
computes the height from them

00:02:10.843 --> 00:02:13.719
the larger of the two children's heights

00:02:13.719 --> 00:02:14.404
plus one.

00:02:14.472 --> 00:02:16.664
The static method height returns

00:02:16.732 --> 00:02:19.266
−1 for an empty tree, that is

00:02:19.266 --> 00:02:22.210
for null, and the stored height otherwise.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.104
With −1 for the empty tree

00:02:25.104 --> 00:02:27.349
one formula works everywhere

00:02:27.349 --> 00:02:29.669
a leaf has two empty children

00:02:29.669 --> 00:02:32.438
so its height is minus one plus one

00:02:32.438 --> 00:02:33.560
which is zero.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.340
To print a tree on one line

00:02:36.419 --> 00:02:39.327
we write a node's key followed by its

00:02:39.405 --> 00:02:42.235
left and right subtrees in brackets.

00:02:42.235 --> 00:02:45.300
Here: 20, then the subtree at 10

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.500
then the subtree at 30.

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:49.973
10 has a left child 5

00:02:50.058 --> 00:02:51.672
and no right child

00:02:51.672 --> 00:02:55.154
and a dash stands for the empty subtree.

00:02:55.154 --> 00:02:56.682
30 is a leaf

00:02:56.682 --> 00:02:59.570
so it is written as just its key.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.914
Every program in the notes prints

00:03:01.975 --> 00:03:03.123
its trees this way

00:03:03.123 --> 00:03:05.480
so you can redraw each result by hand.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.692
So why not keep the tree complete

00:03:08.772 --> 00:03:10.288
after every update

00:03:10.288 --> 00:03:13.080
with the smallest possible height?

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:16.270
Take the complete tree 6, 4, 9

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:18.903
with leaves 1, 5 and 8.

00:03:18.903 --> 00:03:19.860
Insert 2.

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:24.152
The keys are now 1, 2, 4, 5, 6

00:03:24.152 --> 00:03:25.343
8, 9

00:03:25.435 --> 00:03:28.643
and the only complete binary search

00:03:28.735 --> 00:03:31.210
tree on them has root 5.

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:34.418
The root changes from 6 to 5

00:03:34.418 --> 00:03:37.810
and every node changes its position.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.449
In a complete tree the shape

00:03:40.531 --> 00:03:42.166
is fixed by n alone

00:03:42.247 --> 00:03:44.373
so one insertion can force

00:03:44.454 --> 00:03:46.335
us to move all n nodes.

00:03:46.335 --> 00:03:48.869
That is Θ(n) per update

00:03:48.869 --> 00:03:50.994
no better than the chain.

00:03:51.076 --> 00:03:53.283
The way out is to relax the

00:03:53.365 --> 00:03:54.918
condition a little.

00:03:54.918 --> 00:03:57.779
We don't need the smallest height.

00:03:57.779 --> 00:04:00.640
Any height in O(log n) will do.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:04.685
A family of binary search trees that keeps the

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:07.848
height in O(log n) after every update

00:04:07.848 --> 00:04:10.259
at O(log n) cost per update

00:04:10.259 --> 00:04:12.820
is called a balanced search tree.

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:14.703
AVL trees, this lecture

00:04:14.778 --> 00:04:17.414
keep the two subtrees of every node

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:19.900
within height one of each other.

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:23.126
Red-black trees, in Lecture 16

00:04:23.126 --> 00:04:26.561
colour the nodes and keep colouring rules.

00:04:26.561 --> 00:04:29.197
They are slightly less balanced

00:04:29.197 --> 00:04:31.035
but cheaper to update.

00:04:31.115 --> 00:04:33.272
Splay trees have no balance

00:04:33.352 --> 00:04:34.710
condition at all

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:37.266
every access moves the accessed

00:04:37.346 --> 00:04:38.704
node to the root

00:04:38.704 --> 00:04:41.900
which gives O(log n) amortised time.

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:45.095
And B-trees put many keys in each node.

00:04:45.095 --> 00:04:48.690
Databases and file systems use them on disk.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:51.659
The AVL tree is the oldest of them.

00:04:51.733 --> 00:04:54.502
It was invented in 1962

00:04:54.577 --> 00:04:56.746
by two Soviet mathematicians

00:04:56.746 --> 00:04:58.766
Adelson-Velsky and Landis

00:04:58.766 --> 00:05:01.385
and is named after their initials.

00:05:01.385 --> 00:05:03.480
Let's see what it asks for.

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:05.670
Two numbers per node.

00:05:05.747 --> 00:05:09.275
The height of a node is the number of edges on

00:05:09.351 --> 00:05:12.419
the longest path from it down to a leaf.

00:05:12.419 --> 00:05:14.873
Here are the heights in tree a

00:05:14.873 --> 00:05:18.170
the leaves have 0, node 11 has 1

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:19.704
node 8 has 2

00:05:19.704 --> 00:05:21.851
and the root 6 has 3.

00:05:21.851 --> 00:05:23.308
And by convention

00:05:23.308 --> 00:05:26.146
the empty tree has height -1

00:05:26.146 --> 00:05:29.060
like the missing right child of 4.

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:32.060
The balance factor of a node is the

00:05:32.136 --> 00:05:34.861
height of its left subtree minus the

00:05:34.937 --> 00:05:37.057
height of its right subtree.

00:05:37.132 --> 00:05:39.479
A positive balance factor means

00:05:39.555 --> 00:05:41.296
the node is left-heavy

00:05:41.296 --> 00:05:43.416
a negative one right-heavy.

00:05:43.416 --> 00:05:46.596
4 has a leaf on the left, height 0

00:05:46.596 --> 00:05:49.927
and nothing on the right, height -1

00:05:49.927 --> 00:05:51.290
balance +1.

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:54.645
8 has the leaf 7 on the left

00:05:54.729 --> 00:05:58.151
and the subtree at 11, of height 1

00:05:58.151 --> 00:06:01.824
on the right: 0 - 1, so -1.

00:06:01.824 --> 00:06:05.497
Every node of tree a has balance -1

00:06:05.497 --> 00:06:07.000
0 or +1.

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:09.682
That is the definition.

00:06:09.773 --> 00:06:13.794
An AVL tree is a binary search tree in which

00:06:13.885 --> 00:06:17.541
every node has balance factor -1

00:06:17.541 --> 00:06:19.186
0 or +1.

00:06:19.186 --> 00:06:20.648
For every node

00:06:20.739 --> 00:06:23.572
the heights of its two subtrees

00:06:23.663 --> 00:06:25.674
differ by at most one.

00:06:25.674 --> 00:06:28.324
Tree a passes at every node

00:06:28.324 --> 00:06:30.700
so tree a is an AVL tree.

00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:34.025
So an AVL tree has three properties

00:06:34.108 --> 00:06:36.505
at most two children per node

00:06:36.587 --> 00:06:38.488
and the order property

00:06:38.488 --> 00:06:41.464
both as for any binary search tree

00:06:41.464 --> 00:06:43.778
plus the balance condition.

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:46.588
If an update ever makes a balance

00:06:46.670 --> 00:06:49.067
factor +2 or -2

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:51.794
the tree must be repaired before

00:06:51.877 --> 00:06:53.530
the update finishes.

00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:56.273
Tree b is built the same way

00:06:56.273 --> 00:06:59.479
from nine keys, and it is not an AVL tree.

00:06:59.554 --> 00:07:01.717
The program prints a table of

00:07:01.791 --> 00:07:03.879
heights and balance factors

00:07:03.879 --> 00:07:06.713
and three rows are marked unbalanced.

00:07:06.713 --> 00:07:09.398
Node 1 has an empty left subtree

00:07:09.398 --> 00:07:10.740
height -1

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:13.648
and on the right the subtree at 3

00:07:13.648 --> 00:07:14.767
of height 1.

00:07:14.767 --> 00:07:17.750
-1 - 1: balance -2.

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:21.838
Node 4 has a left subtree of height 2

00:07:21.927 --> 00:07:25.042
and a right subtree of height 0

00:07:25.042 --> 00:07:25.932
+2.

00:07:26.021 --> 00:07:28.602
And the root 6 has subtrees

00:07:28.691 --> 00:07:32.340
of heights 3 and 1: +2 again.

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:34.521
The other nodes are fine.

00:07:34.521 --> 00:07:36.278
Of the three offenders

00:07:36.278 --> 00:07:38.109
the deepest is node 1.

00:07:38.109 --> 00:07:41.184
Remember that node: when we repair trees

00:07:41.258 --> 00:07:43.235
the deepest unbalanced node

00:07:43.308 --> 00:07:45.212
is where the work happens.

00:07:45.212 --> 00:07:46.530
That is part 2.

00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:47.938
Your turn.

00:07:47.938 --> 00:07:51.251
On the left, the chain 1, 2, 3.

00:07:51.251 --> 00:07:54.646
On the right, the tree with root 2 and

00:07:54.729 --> 00:07:56.634
children 1 and 3.

00:07:56.634 --> 00:07:58.787
Is the chain an AVL tree?

00:07:58.787 --> 00:08:01.769
If not, which node breaks the rule

00:08:01.769 --> 00:08:04.419
and what is its balance factor?

00:08:04.419 --> 00:08:07.897
And is the tree on the right an AVL tree?

00:08:07.897 --> 00:08:10.630
Pause the video and work it out.

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:16.036
Work from the bottom up.

00:08:16.036 --> 00:08:18.546
The leaf 3 has balance 0.

00:08:18.546 --> 00:08:21.133
Node 2 has nothing on the left

00:08:21.133 --> 00:08:22.502
height -1

00:08:22.502 --> 00:08:25.012
and the leaf 3 on the right

00:08:25.012 --> 00:08:27.447
height 0: balance -1.

00:08:27.447 --> 00:08:28.740
That is allowed.

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:31.326
Node 1 has nothing on the left

00:08:31.326 --> 00:08:34.293
and the subtree at 2, of height 1

00:08:34.293 --> 00:08:36.956
on the right: −1 − 1

00:08:36.956 --> 00:08:38.477
which is −2.

00:08:38.477 --> 00:08:40.987
So the chain is not an AVL tree

00:08:40.987 --> 00:08:43.650
and node 1 is the only offender.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:46.467
The tree on the right has balance

00:08:46.541 --> 00:08:49.606
0 at every node, so it is an AVL tree

00:08:49.606 --> 00:08:51.400
and even a perfect one.

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:53.660
Two classic mistakes.

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:56.620
The first is checking only the root.

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:00.140
A root with two long chains of equal length

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:02.940
hanging below it has balance 0

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:05.980
and the tree is far from an AVL tree.

00:09:05.980 --> 00:09:08.780
The condition is about every node.

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:10.948
The second is giving the

00:09:11.024 --> 00:09:12.766
empty tree height 0.

00:09:12.766 --> 00:09:15.720
An empty subtree has height −1.

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:16.705
So node 2

00:09:16.705 --> 00:09:19.734
with one leaf child and no other child

00:09:19.734 --> 00:09:22.234
has balance −1, not 0.

00:09:22.234 --> 00:09:23.900
Count with −1

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:26.930
and the balance factors come out right.

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:28.341
To sum up.

00:09:28.341 --> 00:09:31.748
One: a binary search tree costs O(h)

00:09:31.748 --> 00:09:34.906
and h can be as large as n − 1.

00:09:34.989 --> 00:09:37.565
Keeping the tree complete would

00:09:37.648 --> 00:09:39.892
cost Θ(n) per update.

00:09:39.892 --> 00:09:43.299
Two: the balance factor of a node is the

00:09:43.382 --> 00:09:46.373
height of its left subtree minus the

00:09:46.456 --> 00:09:48.783
height of its right subtree

00:09:48.783 --> 00:09:52.190
with the empty tree at height −1.

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:53.059
Three

00:09:53.146 --> 00:09:56.954
an AVL tree is a binary search tree in which

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:00.502
every node has balance factor −1

00:10:00.502 --> 00:10:02.060
0 or +1.

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:03.390
Try this.

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:08.071
Insert 8, 4, 12, 2, 6, 10

00:10:08.071 --> 00:10:12.861
1, 3 into a plain binary search tree.

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:15.800
Give every node its height

00:10:15.909 --> 00:10:17.977
and balance factor.

00:10:17.977 --> 00:10:20.046
Is it an AVL tree?

00:10:20.046 --> 00:10:22.005
Then insert 0.

00:10:22.005 --> 00:10:25.380
Which nodes become unbalanced

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:28.210
and which is the deepest?

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:31.830
In part two: why an AVL tree is never

00:10:31.919 --> 00:10:35.542
much taller than log n, and the rotation

00:10:35.542 --> 00:10:37.840
the tool that repairs it.

